"""The mobile brief text: an argument table plus short rules (fix/1570 B18).

It rides once per chat in the first mobile reply (joined in ``tools.tool_briefs``).
Prose that only restated the tool schema or the step format is gone; every rule
the host needs is still here, one line per argument or op. The ``@@..@@`` tokens
are filled by ``tool_briefs`` from ``tools.mobile.path_plan_text`` (and ``@@NO_FABRICATION@@`` from ``guard_consts``). Leaf module.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

MOBILE_BRIEF_TEXT = """\
MOBILE RULES (qa_mobile_test / qa_submit_mobile_step / qa_mobile_status)

ANY action on an Android emulator or device (install, launch, switch environment, log in, tap, type, explore) goes through qa_mobile_test. For ad-hoc steps pass goal="..." (apply defaults to true; no suite or cases needed): it drives the device step by step. Never use raw adb or shell commands for device actions (adb shell input/am/pm, uiautomator dumps, ADB Keyboard broadcasts): the tool owns the destructive guard, keyboard install/restore, the run folder, evidence and screenshots, and a raw command skips all of them. @@NO_FABRICATION@@

START: call qa_mobile_test with NO arguments. It answers with what the machine needs next (setup guide, install source, preflight list or start menu) and asks the tester itself. Every step that installs, downloads or launches needs apply=true; once the tester asked for an install, pass apply=true on the first call. With no Android SDK or AVD it answers `setup_required: true`: relay its steps (this server downloads no SDK; it can list, boot, create and delete AVDs, see `emulator` below). It hands you ONE packet at a time; answer each with qa_submit_mobile_step.

qa_mobile_test ARGUMENTS
- goal: the task, on the FIRST call. A charter-less explore with no goal gets a CHARTER INTAKE packet: put its questions (goal, depth, scope, destructive, budget) to the tester in your own words, ask no others, call again with `charter` as a JSON object using the packet's field names. Never put a password, OTP or personal value in it: it is written to the report. Defaults used are named in the report.
- charter: `destructive` defaults to "none" and REFUSES every irreversible op; a goal that reads like a reset gets a hint to resend with "reversible". An out-of-enum field (e.g. depth: "functional") is never silently accepted: the reply names the field, what was sent and used, and a JSON block with the real `enum` values.
- source + app: `source` is an install-menu key (value in `app`: path, URL or package name; for source=installed_package `package` also works and wins if both are given). The run menu (current_suite, stored_suite, own_cases, explore, rerun_failures, resume) is a SEPARATE question answered by the same `source` in a later call, once the app is installed; or pass goal/cases/suite_id and skip it.
- device_id (alias serial; two different values are refused): an already running emulator, skips the AVD lookup. avd: which AVD to boot when none runs and several exist. Leave both empty to use the one booted device.
- run_id: continue a run in ANY chat (takes it over, the old chat is told). If the same cases are an unfinished run of the same app the reply gives THAT run's id; new_run=true only when the tester asked for a fresh run.
- launch: default opens the app before the first packet, also when the goal asks to confirm the home screen first (send a `home` op to observe it). launch=false ONLY when the user said the app must NOT be opened: the reply says "App NOT opened"; reset_app and scripted case runs still open it.
- reset_app=true: clears the app's data and relaunches it (needs a live run, never touches another app). Same as a `clear_app_data` script op; do not resend that op if the first packet says data was cleared at run start.
- grant_permissions (default true): grants the app's declared permissions (incl. notifications) before step one; false only when the run tests the permission prompt itself. deny_permissions=[names] (location, camera, microphone, notifications, contacts, photos/storage, phone, sms, calendar, nearby/bluetooth) denies only those. An unclear pop-up stops the run as `permission_unclear` for you to answer.
- locale (`ar`, `ar-EG`, `en-US`; needs apply=true): sets the device language before install, read back and shown in the report header. If the device will not take it the call REFUSES and says what the device is in; on an emulator not booted by this server `persist.sys.locale` usually needs root and the refusal says how to set it by hand.
- env="name=value,..." declares the setup (e.g. backend=staging) so env-scoped app notes apply; a script may carry knowledge_feedback.
- emulator: `list` (read-only), `boot` (avd=NAME, apply=true), `create` (avd=NAME, system_image= an installed id from `list`, apply=true; downloads nothing, relay the steps if none), `delete` (avd=NAME, apply=true AND confirm_destructive=true, only after the TESTER says yes). Names: letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`, not starting with `.` or `-`. Never run avdmanager, sdkmanager or emulator in a shell. If the computer cannot run the emulator (no hypervisor) relay the fix and suggest a real device.

qa_submit_mobile_step: the server validates the script against the action vocabulary, replays it on the device and returns the verdict plus the NEXT packet.
- Credentials: ask the TESTER for the one field; pass tester_input with tester_input_field. Two or more fields in one turn: tester_inputs='{"login_password": "...", "login_otp": "..."}'. They are typed into the app and stored nowhere (not in the report, checkpoint or audit log). In the script use {"op": "type", "target": ..., "field": "login_password", "secret": true}. Example: type ID, type password, tap send, wait_until_text for the code screen, type the code, assert, done, all in ONE script. The final assert and done belong in the same script as the step before.
- One script carries SEVERAL actions, replayed in order until one needs a screen re-read or the case ends (the Example at the end is ONE submit, not three). @@PATH_PLAN@@ @@STOP_NOTE@@ @@PERMISSION_NOTE@@ A script that carries exactly one action still runs, but the reply says so.
- fill: {"op": "fill", "label": "Email", "text": "user@example.com"} beats tap then type: it finds the field by hint, text or description (or nearest below/right of a matching label), taps, types and checks. A plain fill needs `text`; `field` is read only for a secret fill ("secret": true, `field` names the tester input, never the value). Two matching fields: refused with the candidates, resend with a narrower label.
- Waits: wait FOR something (wait_until_text, wait_until_gone, wait_until_changed, wait_until_idle), never a number, and never sleep in a shell: nobody watches the phone. `wait` takes ms, `until_text`, or `until_rid` (present) with `until_gone=true` (gone) or `until_rid_text` (its text or description contains it); until_text and until_rid never go together.
- confirm_destructive=true, only after the TESTER confirms a guard stop: it unlocks only the control THIS case's last stop named. A different op or element, or no stop recorded, refuses by name and does not spend it. destructive: none still refuses.
- flow / save_flow (managed with qa_mobile_flows): `flow`=name replays one, empty `script`, `flow_params` JSON for its {placeholders}. `save_flow`=name saves this `script` only after a clean replay: typed text must be a {placeholder} (value in flow_params), a password a secret step with a `field`; values are never kept. A step that no longer matches stops and hands back to you.
- route / save_route (qa_mobile_flows kind=route): a flow whose every screen is checked; `save_route` remembers the screen each step started on; `route`=name replays ONLY from that screen, any mismatch stops with nothing tapped. For known trips (go to login, switch env to QA): no planning turn.

SCREENS: DUMP FIRST, plan from the element list; screenshots are never sent to you; any picture stays on the server for the report. Coordinates come from the element list. A screen byte-identical to the last one sent is replaced by a one-line same-screen note: your list is still exact. qa_capture_screens(peek=true) returns the current element list as text (nothing tapped or saved, no apply); if it says the list is not enough, call without `peek` to capture and keep them on the server.

qa_mobile_status: emulator, lease holder, cases done/failed/remaining; call it after anything that outlives a tool call (large install, cold boot). No run_id lists the runs here. report_now=true also writes the standalone HTML report (partial mid-run) and returns its path.

A finished run's reply STARTS with a verdict block (verdict, each requested step, the typed-field tally). Relay it to the tester word for word: never upgrade, soften or summarise it.

PACKET RULES: a control labelled Voice, Record or Mic is NEVER the Send control. Short ids describe the screen you were given; a stale one is handed back, never mistapped, but costs a turn. Never send TWO selectors naming different elements, nor one that matches nothing beside one that matches. After a tap that opens a new page the next packet hands you that screen: plan it then (role, rid, label or text); never redo steps that ran. For 'the app replies' assert new_text (optionally with contains) or text_present, NEVER screen_changed. One submit replays a whole script; a shell call replays one action and bypasses the IME, step record and report. No capture, status, app_info, list_devices, search_corpus or get_mcp_tools during a run. done(verdict): 'pass' = you verified the expected results, 'fail' = you verified they did not hold, 'blocked' = you could not tell; guessing 'pass' is the worst answer.
"""
